    title: "Reading Passage",
    level: "B1",
    text: "Music, Food, and the Feeling of Belonging — A Day at the Festival\n\nThe annual Riverside Festival takes over the town park every September. It is a celebration of food, music, and community — and for a language learner, it is a treasure chest of useful vocabulary.\n\nThe park is transformed with decorations. Banners hang between the trees. Bunting — small triangular flags on a string — lines every path. Balloons cluster around the entrance. Streamers twist in the breeze. Lanterns glow as the sun goes down. A garland of paper flowers frames the main stage. Flags from different countries mark the international food section. Ribbons decorate the stalls. Confetti covers the ground near the children's area, where a piñata hangs from a branch, waiting to be broken open. A wreath of autumn leaves decorates the welcome sign.\n\nThe food stalls stretch along the main path. The smell of barbecue fills the air. Kebabs turn on a grill. A pizza slice is served from a wood-fired oven. Tacos, samosas, and spring rolls represent different cuisines. A waffle stand and a crêpe station offer sweet options. Fruit skewers and roasted corn are healthier choices. Doughnuts, fried chicken, and churros attract long queues. For drinks: lemonade, smoothies, hot chocolate, milkshakes, and juice boxes. Cotton candy — spun sugar on a stick — is the children's favourite. Caramel apples glisten in the light. Popcorn pops in a glass machine. Ice cream vans park at both ends.\n\nThe main stage hosts live music all day. A band plays in the afternoon. A singer performs solo in the evening. A DJ booth takes over after dark, and the dance floor fills with people. Speakers carry the sound across the park.\n\nEntertainment is everywhere. A fire dancer spins flaming torches. An acrobat balances on a wire. A stilt walker — standing three metres tall on wooden poles — waves to children. A face painter transforms children into tigers, butterflies, and superheroes. A parade winds through the park with drums, costumes, and dancing. Carnival games line one side — ring toss, duck fishing, and balloon darts. A photo booth captures memories. An arts and crafts tent offers pottery painting and bracelet making. A puppet show draws a crowd of small children.\n\nThe festival brings the whole town together. Neighbours who rarely speak find themselves sharing a bench, eating churros, and watching the fire dancer. Visitors from other cities discover local traditions. Children run between stalls with painted faces and sticky fingers.\n\nA festival is more than entertainment. It is a place where culture, food, music, and language mix freely — and where everyone, no matter where they come from, is welcome.",
